\s1 David Arranges Uriah’s Death
\p \v 14 \w The next morning|strong="H1242"\w* \w David|strong="H1732"\w* \w wrote|strong="H3789"\w* \w a letter|strong="H5612"\w* \w to|strong="H413"\w* \w Joab|strong="H3097"\w* \w and sent|strong="H7971"\w* \w it with Uriah|strong="H223"\w*. \v 15 \w In the letter|strong="H5612"\w* \w he wrote|strong="H3789"\w*: “\w Put|strong="H3051"\w* \w Uriah|strong="H223"\w* \w at|strong="H413"\w* \w the front|strong="H4136"\w* \w of the fiercest|strong="H2389"\w* \w battle|strong="H4421"\w*; \w then withdraw|strong="H7725"\w* \w from him|strong="H310"\w*, \w so that he may be struck down|strong="H5221"\w* \w and killed|strong="H4191"\w*.”
\p \v 16 \w So as|strong="H1961"\w* \w Joab|strong="H3097"\w* \w besieged|strong="H8104"\w* \w the city|strong="H5892"\w*, \w he assigned|strong="H5414"\w* \w Uriah|strong="H223"\w* \w to|strong="H413"\w* \w a place|strong="H4725"\w* \w where|strong="H834"\w* \w he knew|strong="H3045"\w* \w the strongest enemy soldiers were|strong="H582"\w*. \v 17 \w And when the men|strong="H582"\w* \w of the city|strong="H5892"\w* \w came out|strong="H3318"\w* \w and fought|strong="H3898"\w* \w against|strong="H854"\w* \w Joab|strong="H3097"\w*, \w some of|strong="H4480"\w* \w David’s|strong="H1732"\w* \w servants|strong="H5650"\w* \w fell|strong="H5307"\w*, \w and Uriah|strong="H223"\w* \w the Hittite|strong="H2850"\w* \w also|strong="H1571"\w* \w died|strong="H4191"\w*.
\p \v 18 \w Joab|strong="H3097"\w* \w sent|strong="H7971"\w* \w to David|strong="H1732"\w* \w a full account|strong="H3605"\w* \w of the battle|strong="H4421"\w* \v 19 \w and instructed|strong="H6680"\w* \w the messenger|strong="H4397"\w*, “\w When you have finished|strong="H3615"\w* \w giving|strong="H1696"\w* \w the king|strong="H4428"\w* \w a full|strong="H3605"\w* \w account|strong="H1697"\w* \w of the battle|strong="H4421"\w*, \v 20 \w if|strong="H518"\w* \w the king’s|strong="H4428"\w* \w anger|strong="H2534"\w* \w flares|strong="H5927"\w*, \w he may ask|strong="H559"\w* you, ‘\w Why|strong="H4069"\w* \w did you get so close|strong="H5066"\w* \w to|strong="H413"\w* \w the city|strong="H5892"\w* \w to fight|strong="H3898"\w*? \w Did you not|strong="H3808"\w* \w realize|strong="H3045"\w* \w they would shoot|strong="H3384"\w* \w from atop|strong="H5921"\w* \w the wall|strong="H2346"\w*? \v 21 \w Who|strong="H4310"\w* \w struck|strong="H5221"\w* \w Abimelech|strong="H40"\w* \w son|strong="H1121"\w* \w of Jerubbesheth|strong="H3380"\w*\f + \fr 11:21 \fqa Jerubbesheth \ft is also known as \fqa Jerubbaal\ft , that is, Gideon.\f*? \w Was it not|strong="H3808"\w* \w a woman|strong="H802"\w* \w who dropped|strong="H7993"\w* \w an upper millstone|strong="H6400"\w* \w on him|strong="H5921"\w* \w from|strong="H5921"\w* \w the wall|strong="H2346"\w*, \w so that he died|strong="H4191"\w* \w in Thebez|strong="H8405"\w*? \w Why|strong="H4100"\w* \w did you get so close|strong="H5066"\w* \w to|strong="H413"\w* \w the wall|strong="H2346"\w*?’
\p \w If he asks you this, then you are to say|strong="H559"\w*, ‘\w Your servant|strong="H5650"\w* \w Uriah|strong="H223"\w* \w the Hittite|strong="H2850"\w* \w is dead|strong="H4191"\w* \w as well|strong="H1571"\w*.’”
